steps.body:
`JSON.stringify(value)` turns objects and arrays into one line of JSON text. Pass `null` and `2` as extra arguments to pretty-print with 2-space indentation, which is easier to read in logs and files.

```javascript
console.log(JSON.stringify({ a: 1, b: [2, 3] })); // {"a":1,"b":[2,3]}
console.log(JSON.stringify({ a: 1 }, null, 2));
// {
//   "a": 1
// }
```

JSON only knows strings, numbers, booleans, `null`, arrays, and plain objects. Everything else changes on the way out:

- Keys whose value is `undefined` or a function are skipped. Inside an array they become `null` instead.
- `NaN` and `Infinity` become `null`.
- A Map or a Set becomes `{}`, so convert it first with `Object.fromEntries(map)` or `[...set]`.
- A BigInt makes `JSON.stringify` throw a TypeError, so convert it with `String(big)` first.

> Gotcha: apart from the BigInt, none of this is an error. Missing keys and `{}` appear silently, so check what you send.

example.code:
const user = {
  name: "Ada",
  age: 36,
  langs: ["js", "python"],
  nickname: undefined,
  score: NaN,
  greet: () => "hi",
};

console.log(JSON.stringify(user));
console.log(JSON.stringify(user, null, 2));

const visits = new Map([["home", 3], ["about", 1]]);
console.log(JSON.stringify(visits));
console.log(JSON.stringify(Object.fromEntries(visits)));

const big = 12345678901234567890n;
console.log(JSON.stringify({ id: String(big) }));

