steps.body:
Parsed data often has missing parts. `user.address.city` throws a TypeError when `address` is undefined. Optional chaining asks first: `obj?.prop` gives `undefined` instead of throwing when `obj` is `null` or `undefined`.

```javascript harness
const user = { name: "Ada", address: { city: "London" }, tags: ["admin", "dev"] };
const obj = null;
const key = "name";
const format = { upper: (s) => s.toUpperCase() };
{{code}}
```

```javascript
console.log(user.address?.city);   // London, property
console.log(user.tags?.[0]);       // admin, bracket access on an array
console.log(obj?.[key]);           // undefined, bracket access with a variable
console.log(format.upper?.("hi")); // HI, call only if the method exists
```

Pair it with `??` to supply a default: `user.address?.city ?? "unknown"`. Use `??`, not `||`, so valid values such as `0` and `""` are kept.

A chain stops at the first `null` or `undefined` and the whole expression becomes `undefined`. So in `nobody?.address.city`, nothing after `?.` runs when `nobody` is null. Put `?.` only at the steps that can really be missing. Extra `?.` hides bugs in data that should always be there. Also, `?.()` only skips a missing method: if the property holds something that is not a function, the call still throws a TypeError.

example.code:
const text = '[{"name":"Ada","address":{"city":"London"},"tags":["admin","dev"]},{"name":"Linus","tags":[]},{"name":"Grace","address":{"city":null},"tags":null}]';
const users = JSON.parse(text);

function cityOf(user) {
  return user.address?.city ?? "unknown";
}

function firstTag(user) {
  return user.tags?.[0] ?? "none";
}

for (const user of users) {
  console.log(`${user.name}: ${cityOf(user)}, ${firstTag(user)}`);
}

const nobody = null;
console.log(`nobody: ${nobody?.address.city}`);

const format = { upper: (s) => s.toUpperCase() };
console.log(format.upper?.("ok") ?? "no upper");
console.log(format.lower?.("OK") ?? "no lower");

