steps.body:
Spread `{ ...obj }` and `Object.assign({}, obj)` copy only the top level. Nested arrays and objects are still shared, so changing them through the copy changes the original too.

`structuredClone(value)` makes a deep copy: every nested array, object, Map, and Set is copied, and it keeps `undefined` values.

```javascript harness
const original = { name: "settings", nested: { list: [1, 2, 3] } };
{{code}}
console.log(`original: ${original.nested.list.join(", ")}`);
console.log(`copy: ${copy.nested.list.join(", ")}`);
```

```javascript
const copy = structuredClone(original);
copy.nested.list.push(4); // original is untouched
```

It copies data, not classes. A cloned class instance is a plain object with the same public fields, but without its methods or `#private` fields. Cloning a function, or an object that holds one, throws a DataCloneError.

The older trick `JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(value))` also deep-copies plain JSON data, but it drops `undefined`, turns a Map or Set into `{}`, and throws on a BigInt. Prefer `structuredClone`.

example.code:
const settings = { theme: "dark", editor: { fontSize: 14, tabs: ["main.js"] } };
const prefs = { lang: "en", recent: new Set(["a.js"]), draft: undefined };

class Point {
  constructor(x, y) {
    this.x = x;
    this.y = y;
  }

  describe() {
    return `(${this.x}, ${this.y})`;
  }
}

const shallow = { ...settings };
shallow.theme = "light";
shallow.editor.fontSize = 18;

const deep = structuredClone(settings);
deep.editor.tabs.push("util.js");

console.log(`theme: ${settings.theme}`);
console.log(`fontSize: ${settings.editor.fontSize}`);
console.log(`tabs: ${settings.editor.tabs.join(", ")}`);
console.log(`spread shares editor: ${shallow.editor === settings.editor}`);
console.log(`clone shares editor: ${deep.editor === settings.editor}`);

const jsonCopy = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(prefs));
const cloned = structuredClone(prefs);
console.log(`json: ${JSON.stringify(jsonCopy)}, has draft: ${"draft" in jsonCopy}`);
console.log(`clone recent a.js: ${cloned.recent.has("a.js")}, has draft: ${"draft" in cloned}`);

const copy = structuredClone(new Point(3, 4));
console.log(`copy: ${JSON.stringify(copy)}, describe: ${typeof copy.describe}`);

